.TH MODBUS-REPLAY-DUMP 1 "2026-11-14" "pv-suite 0.5.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
modbus-replay-dump \- read, convert and follow a Modbus wire trace
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modbus-replay-dump
.B trace
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B modbus-replay-dump \-\-to json \-o trace.jsonl trace.pvmb
.br
.B modbus-replay-dump \-\-socket
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B modbus-replay-dump \-\-socket path \-\-record trace.pvmb [\-\-quiet]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\
The trace's reader face \(em the ctl/cli sibling shape, minus the
control plane (there is nothing to control: the trace is the record).
Both trace shapes read the same \(em the binary
.B PVMB
blocks (the capture default) or the JSON v1 lines; the first bytes
pick the walk. The byte layout lives in the tool's README.
.PP
The static views (no flags): the summary line (what the trace holds),
the answer index \(em one line per unique (unit, request PDU): the
query, the recorded reply the replay will serve (the read data's hex
words included), the recorded latency, the ask count \(em and the
conversations \(em the per\-connection query\->answer diagram: one
block per connection naming both ends of the pipe (the client's and
the device's peers), one line per exchange (request \-> response,
each packet's source host:port and its #conn:txn pairing key, both
frame numbers, the recorded latency), the concurrent connections
named, the unpaired records marked in place (no answer recorded /
orphan / the raw handshake lines). The value dumps under the lines cap
at 16 words a block \(em a poller's repeats keep the diagram readable
\(em and \fB--values\-cap\fR \fIN|all\fR reshapes the cap
(\fBall\fR the full block); the answer index dumps the whole block
always.
.PP
The converter (\fB--to json|binary \-o OUT\fR): either shape reads,
the other writes \(em the raw bytes are the one truth (the JSON side
re\-derives the decoded echo, never copies it), so a
binary\->json\->binary round trip lands byte\-identical; the run
boundaries re\-stamp, a torn tail converts as far as it parsed, the
crc recomputes.
.PP
The live view (\fB--socket\fR [PATH]): the daemon's record feed
(\fB--dump-socket\fR) pushes every record as it lands \(em the
conversations render realtime, every packet line naming its source
host:port and its #conn:txn pairing key; a request prints pending, its
response completes the exchange with the latency. The value dumps (the
address\-prefixed words, the bit strings) ride only with
\fB--values\fR \(em off by default, a busy feed stays one line a
frame (the file views dump the values always), the same
\fB--values\-cap\fR word cap shapes them. Bare \fB--socket\fR
spells the derived default
(/tmp/modbus\-replay\-<listen\-port>.socket).
.PP
The recorder (\fB--record\fR FILE, on the live view): the feed's
bytes land in the file verbatim while the conversations render \(em
the header block at connect, then one length\-framed crc\-checked
block per record, flushed per record (a killed recorder leaves a
readable trace). The write is the wire's bytes, one copy: a record the
file holds is byte\-identical to the one the daemon's own
\fB--capture\fR writes, and any family reader takes the file for
captured (the views, the converter, \fBmodbus-replay --replay\fR).
The file is 0600 and appends across recorder sessions \(em the feed
re\-stamps its header per subscriber connect, the session boundary a
re\-connected recorder appends. An unbounded record grows too:
\fB--trace\-max\-mb\fR rotates it at that size, keeping
\fB--trace\-files\fR backups \(em the backups shift
(.1 <\- .2 <\- ... <\- .N, the oldest dropped), the file
reopens fresh and stamps a header of its own (the feed's posture, now),
the received records keep landing verbatim and a block never straddles
two files \(em every backup a complete trace, any reader takes each
for captured. The feed is the family's one record
contract: the cache's trace\-socket subscribes the same way, so the
cache's live wire records to a file with
\fB--socket /tmp/modbus\-cache\-<listen\-port>\-trace.socket
\-\-record trace.pvmb\fR. \fB--quiet\fR drops the per\-record
render \(em the recorder runs silent, the banners and the save tally
stay.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B trace
the trace file (the binary PVMB blocks or the JSON v1 lines — the first
bytes pick the walk)
.TP
.B \-\-socket
the daemon's record feed (\-\-dump\-socket): the conversations render
live; bare \-\-socket spells the derived default
(/tmp/modbus\-replay\-<listen\-port>.socket) — any family feed
subscribes (the cache's trace\-socket included)
.TP
.B \-\-record
the live view saves the feed to this trace file while it renders — the
received PVMB blocks land verbatim (0600, appended across sessions), a
trace any reader takes for captured
.TP
.BI \-\-trace\-max\-mb " MB"
rotate the recorded trace at this size in MB, keeping N backups; 0 = no
size rotation (the default — the recorder appends unbounded). At the
size the backups shift (.1 <\- .2 <\- ... <\- .N, the oldest dropped),
the file reopens fresh and stamps a new header — the fresh record keeps
the plain name, every backup a complete trace (rides \-\-record)
.TP
.BI \-\-trace\-files " N"
rotated trace backups to keep (the size rotation's .1 .. .N; 1 = only
the shift\-away previous file survives) (rides \-\-record)
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
the live view renders nothing per record — the recorder's silent face
(the banners and the save tally stay)
.TP
.B \-\-values
the live view: dump the values under the completed exchanges (the
address\-prefixed words, the bit strings) — off by default, a busy feed
stays one line a frame (the file views always dump them)
.TP
.BI \-\-values\-cap " N|all"
the word cap for the values dumps that cap (the conversations, the live
view's \-\-values dump; the answer index dumps the full block always) —
the default 16 keeps the diagram readable, all dumps the whole block, an
integer >= 1 spells the cap
.TP
.B \-\-to
convert the trace to the other shape (with \-o OUT)
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-out
the converted trace's path (0600)


.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
the live view ends cleanly \(em no traceback, a zero exit; the file
views never wait.

.SH SECURITY
The trace is 0600 at rest and belongs with the secrets: the wire
carries values and names, a Waveshare capture carries the connection
password raw, a suite\-auth capture the PVA1 proof (a leaked trace
can replay it at any server holding the key). The record feed is a
read\-only push on a unix socket \(em no verbs, no tokens, nothing a
command could ride; a subscriber that cannot keep up is dropped (the
trace file has everything).

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B none
the dump reads its flags only \(em the daemon's env twins (DUMP_TYPE,
DUMP_SOCKET) live on
.BR modbus-replay (1).

.SH FILES
.TP
.I the trace file
the wire dump: 0600 \(em the binary PVMB blocks (the capture default)
or the JSON v1 lines (\fB--dump-type json\fR); both shapes read the
same. The recorded trace (\fB--record\fR) rides the same shapes \(em
binary, 0600, appended across sessions; the size rotation's backups
ride beside it as
.IR trace.pvmb.1 " .. " trace.pvmb.N
(each a complete trace).
.TP
.I /tmp/modbus\-replay\-<listen\-port>.socket
the daemon's record feed (\fB--dump-socket\fR) \(em bare
\fB--socket\fR spells this default; any family feed subscribes (the
cache's trace\-socket at
/tmp/modbus\-cache\-<listen\-port>\-trace.socket included).

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success (the live view: ^C ends it cleanly).
.TP
.B 1
a refusal \(em a foreign trace, a missing source, a bad combination
(reason on stderr).
.TP
.B 2
command-line misuse.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR modbus-replay (1)
.PP
\fBapps/modbus-replay/README.md\fR in the suite checkout \(em the
trace format's byte layout, the feed's story.
